# Caddy vhost for the Go RomM server (on ~/llm-stack/caddy/Caddyfile, the
# ai-monster host). Two ways to use it:
#
# 1) Parallel (safe) — reach the Go server at a NEW hostname while the Python
#    container keeps serving romm.ai-monster.local unchanged:
#
#        romm-go.ai-monster.local {
#            reverse_proxy retro.local:8099
#        }
#
# 2) Cutover — point the real hostname at the Go server:
#
#        romm.ai-monster.local {
#            reverse_proxy retro.local:8099   # was the romm container :80
#        }
#
# Then: reload Caddy (caddy reload / systemctl reload caddy). Roll back by
# restoring the previous reverse_proxy target and reloading.
#
# Feature parity as of the golang-rewrite branch: the Go server is a drop-in for
# the whole app — browse, search, play (EmulatorJS), favorites, collections,
# saves/states sync, settings, user management, achievements, music, metadata,
# and the full virtual-download feature (queue, merge, exclude, sources, the
# archive.org scraper). The ONE feature it does not carry is remote-session
# STREAMING (the session-host that streams real emulator processes over WebRTC);
# it is reported disabled, and play happens in-browser via EmulatorJS instead.
# Make sure the service has ROMM_LIBRARY_ROMS_DIR set (see romm-go.service) or
# ROM download/play will 404 after the cutover.
